---
<Article h1={"8-week dead hang program."} subtitle={"Two blocks, three sessions a week, one change at a time. For a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold. No promised hold time."} crumbs={[{"name":"Training plans","href":"/training-programs/"},{"name":"8 weeks"}]} updated={"Updated November 2, 2026"} title="8-Week Dead Hang Program: Weekly Schedule & Reviews" description="A free 8-week dead hang program for a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold: weekly schedule table, 3 sessions a week, review points and a local training log." schema={`<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"8-Week Dead Hang Program: Weekly Schedule & Reviews","description":"A free 8-week dead hang program for a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold: weekly schedule table, 3 sessions a week, review points and a local training log.","dateModified":"2026-11-02","mainEntityOfPage":"https://deadhangs.com/8-week-program/","author":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">${faqLd}</script>`}>
<div class="article-body">
<div class="callout"><h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2><ol class="stop-steps"><li><strong>Who it suits:</strong> a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold. Under that, use the <a href="/4-week-program/">4-week program</a>.</li><li><strong>Schedule:</strong> 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days, 3 sets, 60 to 90 seconds of rest.</li><li><strong>Structure:</strong> two four-week blocks. Change one variable at a time.</li><li><strong>Review:</strong> compare like-for-like sessions at the end of week 4 and week 8.</li></ol><p>These doses are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p></div>
<h2>What is the 8-week dead hang program?</h2>
<p>The 8-week dead hang program is a two-block plan for people who hold a comfortable 20 to 30 seconds. Block one sets a repeatable routine of 3 sessions a week. Block two changes one variable and compares the two blocks. It organizes practice and does not promise a particular hold time.</p>
<p>For one session rather than a multi-week plan, see the <a href="/deadhang-guide/#workout-plan">dead hang workout plan</a> in the guide.</p>
<h2>8-week dead hang program schedule</h2>
<p>The table sets out each two-week step. Use a securely fitted bar and a stable way to step down. Choose an assisted or unassisted hold you can control without pain, and keep that variation consistent when comparing results. If you have an injury or concerns about the movement, seek individual guidance.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="8-week dead hang program schedule" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Weeks</th><th scope="col">Focus</th><th scope="col">Sets and holds</th><th scope="col">Rest between sets</th><th scope="col">What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(r=><tr><td><strong>{r[0]}</strong></td><td>{r[1]}</td><td>{r[2]}</td><td>{r[3]}</td><td>{r[4]}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="session-structure">What does a typical week look like?</h2>
<p>A typical week in the 8-week program has three hang sessions on non-consecutive days and four rest days. Start with Monday, Wednesday and Friday. Add a session only if you stay pain free. Keep a familiar hold duration and support level before trying to add work.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Example week" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Session</th><th scope="col">What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody>{week.map(r=><tr><td><strong>{r[0]}</strong></td><td>{r[1]}</td><td>{r[2]}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>The example week is a starting point you adjust from your own baseline. Step down a second or two before your grip gives out, and do not hang to failure.</p>
<ol><li>Repeat your setup and do a short warm-up of arm circles, wrist circles and one or two easy hangs. The <a href="/mobility/">mobility guide</a> has the full routine.</li><li>Run your chosen hang, rest and set intervals. Record early finishes honestly.</li><li>Review two comparable sessions before changing the setup. Choose one adjustment: duration, assistance, sets or rest.</li><li>Keep the other variables stable through the next review. Return to the earlier settings if control worsens.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Example review:</strong> two sessions use the same bar and three 15-second supported holds. In session three you extend the hold to 18 seconds and keep assistance and rest unchanged. The example demonstrates a comparison method; it does not establish an optimal increase or guarantee progress.</p>
<h2>How do you track the 8-week program?</h2>
<p>The training log on this site tracks the 8-week program on your own device. Enter your own hang duration, rest duration and number of sets, then save the date, assistance, best actual hold and number of sets. Compare supported hangs with supported hangs and unassisted hangs with unassisted hangs.</p>
<a class="dh-btn" href="/training-log/">Open the timer &amp; log</a>
<h2>When should you change the plan?</h2>
<p>Change the 8-week plan when a hold is painful or when you cannot step down under control. Stop a painful hold and end a set before your control goes. A missed session does not require making up extra sets. Repeating a block is an option, and longer holds are not the only reason to keep a record.</p>
<h2>How do you review the block?</h2>
<p>Review the block by comparing your current hold with an earlier hold made under the same conditions. That comparison with your own record is the number that matters for this plan. The <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal calculator</a> shows the difference without inventing a population percentile.</p>
<p>When the eight weeks end, the <a href="/12-week-program/">12-week program</a> suits a comfortable hold of 30 seconds or longer. Dead hangs are one part of a wider routine; the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/physical-activity-guidelines-for-adults-aged-19-to-64/">NHS activity guidance</a> covers broader strength and aerobic activity.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>{faqs.map(f=><div class="faq"><h3>{f.q}</h3><p>{f.a}</p></div>)}
</div>
</Article>
